WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.440
- Hello, and thank you
for joining me today.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.780
I'm Mine Çetinkaya-Rundel

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:12.170
I am a developer educator at
Posit and a professor of the

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.030
practice at Duke University.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.520
I mainly teach statistics
and data science.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.900
And today's agenda is teaching
the tidyverse in 2023.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.100
I am going to talk to you
a little bit about the code base

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.070
that has changed in the
tidyverse over the last year.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:31.370
And, really, when I say last
year, I mean the academic year.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:34.460
And there's an accompanying blog
post to this video that

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.310
you are welcomed and encouraged to
read through for more details as well.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.390
But before we get started
with the tidyverse,

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.100
let me start with a little
bit of a trick for you. So

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.450
this is usually what my RStudio
IDE looks like when I get

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.610
started teaching. This
is the color scheme,

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.860
the theme I prefer to use

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:57.620
when I am working on
my own during the day.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.540
This is what I prefer
when I'm working at night.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.360
And this is what I
prefer when I'm teaching.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.730
And the way I've changed
through these different themes

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.280
is I've written a package
that has a bunch of--

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.900
like, a personal package that has
some add-ins for me so I can quickly

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.870
switch between
teaching mode and

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:21.100
themes that I like to use when
I am actually working on my own.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.430
I recommend getting some shortcuts
for yourself like this as well.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:28.590
So we are going to
start by loading the tidyverse and

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.950
the palmerpenguins packages that
we're going to use for

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.570
some of our data examples.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.080
And I'm going to load two
other packages, gt and package load.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.390
We're going to use these for just
a couple of things along the way.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.050
The first thing I'm going to
talk about is the native pipe.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.840
Your tidyverse pipelines may
have looked something like this

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.460
in the past.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.090
You start with a data frame
and then you pipe that into, say,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.100
a dplyr function, like count,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.340
and the result looks
something like this.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.840
Since R 4.0, you can now
use the native pipe here as well,

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.840
and you're going to get
absolutely the same answer.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.190
So you might be wondering, why
would I want to do that? Well,

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.880
one of the nice
things about using

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.720
the native pipe is that it's
going to work even outside of

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.060
the tidyverse or outside of
loading the magrittr package as well.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.030
So let's go ahead
and restart R.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:25.770
So now none of my packages
are loaded again. And let's add

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.720
something else,
something like--

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.050
start with the mpg data frame

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:35.250
or maybe even mtcars data
frame. And then let's use a

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.430
base R function,
like summary().

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:42.180
Even though I have none of
the tidyverse packages loaded

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.130
or the magrittr package loaded,

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.180
I'm able to use the
native pipe as well.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.380
So if you're teaching your
students with the native pipe

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.980
and teaching them to
build these tidyverse pipelines,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.370
they can learn that workflow
and apply it beyond working

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.900
with the tidyverse as well,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.330
should they be using
a different package,

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:01.470
or in a different course,

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.940
or at some other point in
their life when they're coding in R.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:07.740
Coming back to the tidyverse,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.190
one of the updates to
the tidyverse was the tidyverse

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:17.040
meta package itself. So
tidyverse 2.0 was released in,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.140
I think, March of this year.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.230
Let's go ahead and restart
our R session one more time,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.590
and let's go ahead and
load the tidyverse package.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.880
We can see that, in the past,

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.110
we would also have to
do something like add the lubridate--

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.330
load the lubridate
package separately,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.620
but we don't really
have to do that anymore.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:37.020
So I'm going to restart.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.510
I'm going to show you that
when I load the tidyverse,

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.510
the lubridate package
is loaded as well.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:46.800
If you have not used the
lubridate package before or

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.230
haven't taught the
lubridate package before,

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:53.460
let's go through its
functionality very, very briefly.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.630
Perhaps

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.210
the most important
thing about it,

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:58.990
as you can tell from the name,

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.680
is that it allows you to
work with dates and times.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.120
And these can be pesky
things to work with.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.870
So let's go ahead
and put today's date.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.510
This is right now a numeric

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.280
variable, as you can see.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.780
Then I can use the
lubridate package's ymd

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.940
function. So that

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.950
stands for year, month, and
day. And it will convert that,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:28.320
so it will parse that text
into a Date object for me.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.490
This probably seems pretty
straightforward, right?

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.490
We could have
probably done this by

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.800
string parsing without the
lubridate package as well.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.570
So let's try something else.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:43.650
Let's make it a little
bit more complicated.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.160
I'm going to put this as a
text field. So I might say

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.650
7/19/2023.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.210
And

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:55.620
we can say that the class
of this new object, the new

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.630
variable we created--

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.700
whoops, I should say today_t--

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.810
is a character.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:05.880
This is in the format month,
day, year. So I'm going to use

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.880
the mdy function.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:15.960
And lubridate will
convert that for me to

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.950
a Date object as well. And we
can check that it does that. So

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.950
mdy(today_t)

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.310
and there we go.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.940
Also, this is, I think, a
little bit more complex. But,

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:35.040
again, we could have easily
parsed this string and used the

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.930
forward slashes as delimiters.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.730
So let's make something
even more complex.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:44.640
Let's create something
like an actual text string.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.000
And I will type
here something like,

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.750
"This video is being recorded on
19 July 2023 at 1 pm ET."

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.790
You can see that

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.790
the class of this is

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:05.190
a character. So how are we
going to parse the date from here?

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:08.790
Doing so with string

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.440
parsing would be a little
bit more complicated.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.240
We would have to catch
that 19 July 2023 there.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.300
That might take a bit
of knowing about regular

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.980
expressions. But what I can do
with lubridate is, I can say

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.960
this is in the order
day, month, and year.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.290
And then there's also
an hour there as well.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:36.290
And it will actually go in
and try to parse out the

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.080
date and time component
from that text string.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:43.520
Now, the thing is the time
zone is incorrect. Time zones are

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:48.420
additionally pesky. So
you would, in this case--

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:49.820
let's see--

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:54.290
actually need to add the
time zone itself. And now we

00:06:54.290 --> 00:06:56.790
actually have the right
time there as well.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:07:02.330
And if I look at the class
of this object that we now created,

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.370
that is a POSIXct object.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:09.140
So lubridate is pretty helpful
for dealing with pesky things

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:10.340
like dates and times,

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:15.370
and now it comes with the
core tidyverse as well.

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:20.820
Next, let's go back to when
we first loaded the tidyverse package.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:24.600
So I am going to restart my
R session and talk a little bit

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.260
about conflict resolution.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:30.060
So what do I mean by
conflict resolution? What I mean is that

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:33.510
when I load the tidyverse
package, you can see that there

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:35.820
is a new--

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.010
or updated message.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:41.250
It tells me that there are
two functions in the core

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.350
tidyverse, the
filter() and lag()

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:48.000
functions in the dplyr package,
which masks the filter()

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.090
and lag() functions,

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.870
so the functions with the
same name in a package that is

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:56.280
already loaded with base R,
the stats package. This message has

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.040
been here for some time now.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:04.560
But what is new in
tidyverse 2.0 is that it specifically

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.330
advertises the conflicted
package as well.

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.810
And you can always get
back to this message,

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.480
even if you're not at the
beginning of your R session,

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.840
with the tidyverse_conflicts()
function,

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.420
which will print out the
end of that message for you.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:23.950
So let's go ahead and
unload that dplyr package.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.690
So we're going to take
out the dplyr package.

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:30.480
And let's try to run this very

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.470
common tidyverse pipeline.

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:36.150
I want to take the penguins
data frame and filter it for

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.799
penguins that are Adelies.

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:40.080
You've probably run
into this issue before.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:43.200
If you haven't ran into
this issue, kudos to you,

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.750
but your students probably
have ran into this.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.840
What's happening here is that

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:52.470
the message that we get is
not that it can't find the filter()

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.770
function. It can't find the
dplyr filter() function,

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.620
but it is able to
find the stats filter() function.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.500
And it's complaining that it

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.440
can't find this
penguins object.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.540
Or actually,

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:09.720
that is probably happening
because we also haven't loaded

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.360
the palmerpenguins, so
let's go back and do that.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:16.030
It is complaining that the
object species is not found,

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:18.190
so it's sort of not
understanding what you're

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.020
asking it to do.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:21.400
The way

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:23.320
base R would resolve this is,

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.500
if you then load the dplyr
package, the filter()

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.180
function becomes available.

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:32.860
And because it's been
loaded after base R,

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:34.000
R

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.910
will just automatically choose
the version of the function

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:40.660
that was last loaded, and
things work just fine.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.950
They work just fine
until they fail.

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:48.610
So if we want to be a little
bit more strict about how we go about things,

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:52.150
in terms of having multiple
packages loaded with the same

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:55.630
function names, we can leverage
the conflicted() package,

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:57.340
which is not in
the core tidyverse,

00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:59.240
but it is a tidyverse package.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.070
So I'm going to go
ahead and load that.

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:05.110
And once I load conflicted(),
I can no longer get away

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:09.670
with just using the last
loaded version of filter().

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.870
It explicitly tells me, look,
there are two packages loaded.

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:16.060
They both have the
filter() function in it.

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:18.340
Tell me which one do
you want me to use.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:24.190
One option is, we can
use the colon-colon notation to say,

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:28.510
every time I use filter(), I
can explicitly say I want you

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:30.730
to use the dplyr version.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:33.970
This is the sort of thing
you might see in a package

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:35.590
development scenario, perhaps,

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.560
where you want to be very clear
about where other functions

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:40.360
from other packages
are coming from.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.660
But I think in a data analysis
scenario, this gets pretty

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:46.030
busy. Especially if
you're teaching R,

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:47.710
especially if you're teaching

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:49.000
dplyr for the first time,

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.820
you're probably going to have
a bunch of filter() statements.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:55.930
And constantly noting them with
dplyr colon colon, while not

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:58.810
noting them for all the
other functions that you're teaching,

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:01.400
I think can get confusing
for the students.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.400
So instead, you can

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.500
explicitly declare which
version of filter() you want to

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:11.500
use. And there, you can
say, I want to prefer,

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:15.040
in this R session going
forward, the dplyr version of

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.790
filter(). No more complaints
from the conflicted package.

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:23.320
And I am being very explicit
in my code about which

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.320
version I'm using.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:30.730
So that was all about the
tidyverse meta package. Let's

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:35.020
talk a little bit about now the

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:39.430
improved and expanded
functionality with joins. So,

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:44.530
much of these came with
updates to dplyr with 1.1.0

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:48.100
onwards. I think as of
today, we're at 1.1.2.

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:51.940
So there's been
additional updates to get

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.830
the rough edges sorted.

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:57.790
But generally, these updates are
mainly to better align with

00:11:57.790 --> 00:12:02.560
tools like SQL and
data.table. So we have here

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.100
a sample data frame. So
I've created an island data frame,

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:10.270
where I have noted the
coordinates of the centroid of

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:12.310
these three islands.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:13.980
And what I'm going to do is,

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:18.340
I am going to join the
penguins data frame to this.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:21.990
So I'll take the penguins data
frame and left join it to islands.

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:26.340
And this should be a
pipeline you're sort of familiar with.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:30.640
What we've done in the past
was to use the by argument--

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:35.250
and then in here actually
denoted which variable from the

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:36.510
penguins data frame--

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.190
that's the island variable--

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:41.640
should match to which variable
from the islands data frame--

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.980
that's the name variable.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:48.450
Instead, what we can
do now is, we can say

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:49.530
join_by()--

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.660
so a new function--

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:57.390
and I will say where island
is equal to name. So with

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:01.030
this new notation that
is available to you,

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:05.550
you can actually read this out
loud how we often read out loud

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:06.670
logical statements.

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:10.050
So double equals is something
we would read out loud as

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:11.940
"where x is equal to y."

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:15.670
So now I'm able to say take
the penguins data frame,

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:20.140
left join it to the
islands data frame by

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:25.870
where island is equal to
name. I think that, while the

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:29.890
previous version, the syntax, the
by syntax does not go away,

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:32.470
I would strongly recommend
updating your teaching

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:35.800
materials to use the
join_by() function.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:38.530
And the thing that is
nice about that is that, one,

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:42.970
this reading out loud is made
so much easier and aligned with

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:47.530
how we read out loud
other logical statements. And number two,

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:49.530
when we work with the

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:52.670
older version of this--

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:55.450
so this, we know worked,

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:57.980
but some other versions
worked as well.

00:13:57.980 --> 00:14:01.670
So I could also do
something like this.

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:02.770
And this would work as well,

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:05.860
just because of how
named vectors work in R.

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:10.540
But this wouldn't work because it
says I can't find this new

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:12.380
name that you're talking about.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:15.550
And we also saw at the
beginning that quoting both of

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:17.230
these work as well.

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:21.220
Options are good, but sometimes
they can be confusing. So by

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:25.960
using the join_by() notation, we
no longer need to quote our

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:27.010
variable names,

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:31.310
which aligns much better with
other functions in the tidyverse.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:37.420
Another update to join
functions is where we have

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:41.380
many-to-many relationships.
So I'm going to create a

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:44.980
few more new data frames just
so we have them in our pocket

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:49.720
and we can use them in
our examples. One of them is a

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:53.650
data frame called
three_penguins. And this has

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:58.930
three rows, so data on three penguins.
And we have one row per sample ID.

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:02.260
So imagine that you're a
scientist and you're collecting

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:04.940
data on these penguin samples.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:08.270
So this is what my
three_penguins data frame looks like.

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:10.300
And then we also have--

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:13.150
maybe collected by another
scientist, let's say--

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:16.120
data on weight measurements
of these penguins.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:20.320
And if you have ever done
things like collecting

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:24.160
weight data from animals,

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.980
you probably have done this
thing where you had to measure

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:30.250
at least twice. So we have
this weight_measurements data,

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:32.860
where for each of the
sample ID of penguins,

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:35.540
we have two
measurements as well.

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:37.600
And finally, we have one more,

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:40.780
data on flipper length
measurements of these penguins.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:44.920
So similarly, we've done
two measurements per penguin.

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.100
So let's say we have
data from these three separate scientists

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:50.200
and we want to
bring them together.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:50.770
First,

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:54.610
I'm going to start
by bringing together the three_penguins

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:56.590
data set with

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:58.720
the weight_measurements
data set.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:01.900
And there's just a
one-to-many relationship,

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:05.090
and things just work fine.

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:08.300
However, when I have
many-to-many relationships--

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:08.800
so, remember,

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:12.640
those two scientists that each
take two measurements from

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:14.230
each of the three penguins--

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:17.155
if I try to
join those weight_measurements and

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:22.180
flipper_measurements data
frames from these two

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:25.840
scientists, now
I get a warning.

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:29.680
So I get a warning that
says that it detected many to many

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:34.000
relationships between x and y
that was unexpected. And at the

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:37.600
end of the warning, it
says, if a many-to-many relationship is

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:40.720
expected, you can
set relationship equal to

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:44.260
many-to-many. Whenever I
see a warning like this,

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:46.060
my first hunch is, OK, well,

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:48.910
let's just try what
it tells me to do.

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:50.560
At least it'll shut
up the warning,

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:52.940
but maybe it will also
be the right thing to do.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:54.530
So let's go ahead and add that.

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:57.280
So this relationship
argument is new

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.590
with dplyr 1.1.0

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:04.180
onwards. And, in fact, now
we have explicitly said, yes,

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:08.170
we were expecting
this many-to-many relationship. However,

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.040
let's think about
this for a second.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:13.890
We started with three
penguins, two measurements per penguin,

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:15.540
so six rows of data,

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:18.089
and we're bringing together
these data from these two

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:19.920
scientists. But
all of a sudden,

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:22.410
I have this explosion
of rows situation.

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:25.200
I have 12 rows. And 12
is not a large number,

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:27.810
but imagine if you had hundreds
of measurements from these

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.940
scientists. You would end up
with this explosion of rows.

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:34.470
So in this case, what we
actually should be doing is,

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.660
instead of denoting a
many-to-many relationship,

00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:41.700
we might actually think back to
our data structure and say, no,

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:45.750
I want to join by not
just sample ID but by

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:47.950
measurement ID as well.

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:51.660
So the join_by() function
can take multiple variables,

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:54.150
just like the by
argument of joins can.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:18:00.150
And the relationship argument
of the improved join functions

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:04.140
is great for catching
in the tracks of making

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:06.840
a mistake about how
you're joining data sets.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:09.930
But the suggestions it gives
may not always be the right

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:14.080
suggestion for your use case.
So don't just blindly apply the

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:16.540
warnings. But instead,

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:19.510
think through your data
structure to see how these

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:20.930
should be coming together.

00:18:20.930 --> 00:18:24.100
And I think the teaching tip
to take away from here is that

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:27.100
when you're introducing these
functions to the students,

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:29.530
I would recommend doing
something similar to what I've

00:18:29.530 --> 00:18:31.570
done here, which
is, iteratively,

00:18:31.570 --> 00:18:35.230
sort of fall into the traps
that you expect to fall into or

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:39.760
you don't expect to fall
into. Read the warnings, messages,

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:42.490
or errors out loud. Think
through them with them

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:44.920
together, and then apply

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:48.160
some sort of solutions
to the problem.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:52.660
The explosion of rows problem
is something you have probably

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:54.820
come across working,
particularly,

00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:57.700
with new learners who will
happily join any two data

00:18:57.700 --> 00:18:59.740
frames and sometimes crash

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:02.470
their R session or, if
you're teaching on a teaching server,

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:05.140
crash the whole server. So

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:08.680
walking through these sorts of
examples with small data sets

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.540
where they can see

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:14.590
how the rows are expanding
or contracting back to what we

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:18.890
expect them to be, I
think, is pretty helpful.

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:20.170
Another

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:24.490
new argument is handling
unmatched cases with the

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:25.820
improved join function.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:29.680
So I'm going to make one more
new data frame. This time,

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:33.640
my measurements include
one more penguin.

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:36.130
So I have four penguins
in my data frame.

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:39.070
And we're going to say
take the weight measurements from that

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:42.625
one scientist and left join
them to these four penguins.

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:49.210
It's fine. R will happily join
them for you. But, poof, that

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:51.580
fourth penguin is gone.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:53.680
Maybe you never wanted
the fourth penguin,

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:57.610
and maybe this is exactly
what you wanted. But

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:02.350
it is likely the case that this
was a much larger data set, and

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:06.790
some of these unmatched
cases silently disappeared

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:08.560
and you never caught them.

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:11.450
So one option is to say, well,

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:15.160
I definitely don't care any of
the penguins whose weights I don't have.

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:18.400
So I'm going to explicitly use
an inner join to solve this

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:20.770
problem, not a left join--

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:23.890
so saying that I only want
the penguins that are common

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:30.040
between these two. Another
option is to actually use the

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:34.300
unmatched argument and
say, when I do this join,

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:38.080
anything that's unmatched,
explicitly drop that.

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:38.950
So, again,

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:42.730
the results we're getting from
these is exactly the same. And,

00:20:42.730 --> 00:20:44.650
in fact, you can
also do nothing.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:46.900
You can do exactly what
you did at the beginning,

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:48.500
but do it at your own risk.

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:53.530
So the difference between
using the unmatched argument or

00:20:53.530 --> 00:20:58.660
not is basically saying that
I'm making it very explicit.

00:20:58.660 --> 00:21:00.370
When I come back to this code,

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:04.060
I want to remember that I
explicitly did not care about

00:21:04.060 --> 00:21:06.190
the other penguins whose
weights we did not have.

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:11.950
Another value you can use
for the unmatched argument is

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:15.340
actually error. So this might
be a good starting place,

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:17.940
where it says,

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:20.970
if there's any unmatched
cases, just error out.

00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:24.720
I will stop at that point
and read through the error and then

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:26.050
decide what I want to do.

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:29.850
So the error tells me that
each row of y must be matched

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:34.740
with x. And in this case,
row 4 of y was not matched.

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.140
And now it's
basically telling you,

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:38.760
decide what you
want to do with it.

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:43.380
And you might go back to saying,
fine, drop that one. So the error

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:46.360
value for unmatched is
a great starting place,

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:49.090
particularly for
interactive data analysis.

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:54.090
And then you want to explicitly choose
one of the values and go with that.

00:21:56.280 --> 00:22:00.480
All right, so we've talked
about joins in new dplyr.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:03.690
Let's talk about one other
feature of the most recent

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:07.140
dplyr package, which
is per-operation grouping.

00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:11.760
So I'm going to start with
a very typical tidyverse pipeline here.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:17.080
I want to plot the mean body
weights of penguins by species and sex.

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:19.410
So I'm going to start with
the penguins data set.

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:23.610
Just for ease, I'm going
to drop any of the penguins

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:27.840
for which we don't have body
mass or sex information.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:32.940
And then I am going to
group them by species and sex. So

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:36.810
I can see that this is
now a group data frame.

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:41.430
And then I'm going to
summarize to calculate

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:44.190
the mean body weight

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:46.890
per species and sex
of these penguins.

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:50.910
And then let's go ahead and
actually make a plot of this.

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:53.490
Now, you can see that
when I make this plot--

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.350
or even earlier when I
just did my summary--

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:59.050
there was a message that
got printed out. It says,

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:02.140
summarize() has grouped
output by species.

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:06.230
You can override this using
the ".groups" argument."

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:08.200
So this message--

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:10.390
which can catch
you by surprise,

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:15.220
particularly if you're doing something
like creating a dot plot, where

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:19.240
summarize() is not even
the last statement in your

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:20.530
data pipeline--

00:23:20.530 --> 00:23:24.940
is basically saying that you
had grouped the data by two

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:29.020
variables. When we ran
summarize(), we sort of peeled

00:23:29.020 --> 00:23:31.480
off that last
grouping variable,

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:34.690
but the data are still
grouped by species.

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:38.260
And turns out, for making
this plot, it doesn't make any

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:41.080
difference. So you can happily

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:42.790
ignore that message.

00:23:42.790 --> 00:23:45.730
But what generally tends to
happen is that if we don't

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:49.120
address this issue that the
data frame is still grouped,

00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:53.770
this can result in frustrating
situations, particularly for new learners--

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:56.320
but trust me, even for
experienced users--

00:23:56.320 --> 00:24:00.610
because the data underlying
this is now remain grouping.

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:03.310
And sometimes this can
have downstream effects.

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:05.050
It didn't have
it on the ggplot,

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:07.760
but let's look at
a couple examples.

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:09.790
So here, for example,

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:14.320
I am grouping the data by
species and sex again. I am

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:16.970
going to find the mean
body weights again.

00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:20.450
And then I want the
top row of that output.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:24.880
But if I actually drop my
groups, then the top row of

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:27.010
that output is just one row.

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:28.390
So

00:24:28.390 --> 00:24:32.530
when we have grouping that
persists through the pipeline,

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:36.860
certain functions will then
output slightly different results.

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:41.800
And if you were expecting one and
you get the other or vice versa,

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:45.430
it can be pretty confusing to
try to catch where did this happen.

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:49.390
Another example I like to
give with this is using the

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:52.030
wonderful gt package
for making tables.

00:24:52.030 --> 00:24:54.220
Here, I have

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:58.120
data that is still
grouped after

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:00.050
running the
summarize() function.

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:04.690
So the gt package will
nicely group my output and

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:08.200
display this output for
me as such. However,

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:10.930
when I actually
explicitly drop my groups,

00:25:10.930 --> 00:25:15.190
that grouping structure goes away.
So gt doesn't apply that

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:18.310
nice grouping.
Which one you want?

00:25:18.310 --> 00:25:22.310
The idea is, you probably want
to be explicit about that.

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:27.770
So one of the options that we
saw is that every time you run summarize(),

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:32.110
you can decide what you want to
do with your groups. You can decide

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:35.800
drop them, which means the
remaining data frame is not grouped.

00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:40.150
Or you can decide that you
want to assign another value to

00:25:40.150 --> 00:25:43.450
this, like keep or
keep last, for example.

00:25:43.450 --> 00:25:47.740
Alternatively, with new
dplyr, you have a new option,

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:51.880
per-operation grouping. So
you can see that in this new

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:56.200
pipeline, I do not have a
GROUP BY statement anymore.

00:25:56.200 --> 00:25:59.710
I start with my data frame,
I did the dropping the NAs,

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:04.480
and then I said summarize
to calculate the mean body masses

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:07.640
for me but do that by species.

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:13.750
So this new .by
argument allows me to

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:18.040
do the grouping in the
operation where I am doing the

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:21.520
summarization. And the
resulting data frame is no

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:25.645
longer grouped. And I can
actually do this by species and

00:26:25.645 --> 00:26:28.520
sex, like we were doing
before. And again,

00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:31.120
the resulting data
frame is not grouped.

00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:34.490
I didn't have to explicitly
say, once you're done with it,

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:38.440
drop the groups, which I
think is a slightly less

00:26:38.440 --> 00:26:41.650
straightforward or intuitive way
of going about things.

00:26:41.650 --> 00:26:43.690
But here, we're just
explicitly saying,

00:26:43.690 --> 00:26:46.580
when you're summarizing,
group by these two variables.

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:47.620
And when you're done,

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:50.120
don't leave the data
frame grouped anymore.

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:55.220
So my recommendation would
be, I think, to consider

00:26:55.220 --> 00:26:58.700
teaching the .by version

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:03.260
of this per-operation grouping,
particularly if you find that

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:07.640
your learners tend to
struggle with persistent grouping in

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.020
dplyr pipelines.

00:27:09.020 --> 00:27:12.830
That being said, the new
version of R for

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:15.440
Data Science, which
very recently came out--

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:16.850
right here--

00:27:16.850 --> 00:27:19.490
does mention the .by

00:27:19.490 --> 00:27:22.700
argument for
per-operation grouping,

00:27:22.700 --> 00:27:25.400
but it doesn't dwell
on it too much.

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:29.240
So a majority of
the presentation still uses group_by().

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:32.210
So you can decide based on
how strictly you want to stick to

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:36.980
the text or not if you
are using that text for your teaching.

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:40.370
All right, one
more bit on dplyr--

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:43.370
my most favorite function,
case_when() for some reason.

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:45.480
I really like that function.

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:48.410
So now it has gotten some
updates as well, some

00:27:48.410 --> 00:27:50.780
quality-of-life improvements.

00:27:50.780 --> 00:27:51.900
Let's start with this.

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:55.350
I'm going to calculate some
quantiles for the penguins data

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:57.960
set. So I have the 25th

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:01.740
percentile and the 75th
percentile of my data. And

00:28:01.740 --> 00:28:05.730
using these, I want to
categorize my penguins as

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:07.740
small, medium, or large.

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:12.600
So if a penguin is less than
3,550 grams, I'm going to

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:15.510
label them small. If
they're between these two,

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:17.370
I'm going to label them medium.

00:28:17.370 --> 00:28:19.980
And if they're
greater than 4,750,

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:22.860
I'm going to label them large.

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:26.430
And if they were NAs, I
want to leave them as NAs.

00:28:26.430 --> 00:28:27.900
So here,

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:33.330
what we're seeing is this would
be a typical sort of case when--

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:37.770
pipeline where the last row--

00:28:37.770 --> 00:28:41.070
or the last call says,

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:43.950
if it's not any of these,

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:49.770
then call it true. And for
those rows, label things large.

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:53.280
Let's also do something that
will allow us to more easily

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:55.790
see what's going on
in this pipeline.

00:28:55.790 --> 00:29:00.050
Whenever I have a data frame
that's wide with lots of

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:04.010
columns and the columns
that I'm operating with,

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:07.430
I can't see when I run my
pipeline, I like moving them

00:29:07.430 --> 00:29:12.020
up. So I probably will
want to move up here

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:15.320
the body mass

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:20.400
and the new variable that
I created, the bm_cat variable.

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:24.710
So let's relocate those to
move them up so that I can spot

00:29:24.710 --> 00:29:30.350
check that my assignment is
working well. In fact, we might

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:34.340
temporarily pipe this to the
view() function just to make

00:29:34.340 --> 00:29:37.670
sure that we have some
larges as well. So, indeed,

00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:41.030
in our data frame, we do
have some larges as well.

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:45.380
I have personally always
found explaining why we call

00:29:45.380 --> 00:29:50.720
this true a little
challenging when teaching case_when(). Because

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:54.800
you're not necessarily reading
out all the conditions.

00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:56.490
And even if you do,

00:29:56.490 --> 00:30:00.570
I want to say something like
if all else fails. So case_when()

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:03.660
has gained a new
argument called .default

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:07.260
that basically says, if it's
not any of those, make it

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:11.460
large. So now we
can achieve the same

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:14.820
outcome without using

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:19.260
the true as a catch-all
for anything that didn't match.

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:21.480
And finally, with dplyr,

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:25.320
I want to highlight what
I think is a very exciting

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:29.460
development with if_else(),
case_when(), and friends. So

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:33.030
let's say that I
have my data frame,

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:33.970
something like this,

00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:37.740
and I want to create
a new variable that actually shows

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:40.600
the units, so the grams,

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:42.850
next to the values as well.

00:30:42.850 --> 00:30:46.080
So let's say I want to
have something like this.

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:49.290
For each of the body masses,

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:52.920
I also want to have a
character value that brings together the

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:55.800
value and the units.

00:30:55.800 --> 00:30:59.340
It used to be that you had
to type NA_character_ here.

00:30:59.340 --> 00:31:03.720
Because all the other values
in that column were characters, so

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:05.220
now you have to introduce--

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:10.440
to do this very simple task of
creating a character string from a numeric--

00:31:10.440 --> 00:31:14.670
the notion that there are
different sorts of names in R.

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:17.310
And I think that's a pretty
high-level thing to be

00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:21.450
mentioning. So now you can
actually get away with calling

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:26.610
this NA and don't have to
worry about NA_character_ or other NA variants,

00:31:26.610 --> 00:31:30.270
where I think they don't
necessarily belong as early in

00:31:30.270 --> 00:31:34.350
an introductory R curriculum
as what we have here.

00:31:36.450 --> 00:31:42.870
Next, let's leave dplyr aside and take
a bit of a detour and talk about

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:46.170
tidyr and, specifically,
separating columns.

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:48.240
So I have a new
data frame here,

00:31:48.240 --> 00:31:51.180
data on penguins with
text string descriptions.

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:53.550
So maybe you have a
different scientist who,

00:31:53.550 --> 00:31:55.550
instead of putting
things in a spreadsheet,

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:58.160
has put them in a Word
document or something.

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:01.340
And you have these

00:32:01.340 --> 00:32:04.940
three specimens and
some descriptions about them.

00:32:04.940 --> 00:32:08.270
And what you might want to
do is take these descriptions and

00:32:08.270 --> 00:32:13.640
turn them into a data frame
or a spreadsheet version.

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:18.350
The separate() function
existed previously in

00:32:18.350 --> 00:32:19.370
tidyr,

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:24.690
but it has evolved to
allow for different ways of separating.

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:28.850
So one option is to separate.
And we want to separate

00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:32.060
wider because we want to
put these pieces of information in

00:32:32.060 --> 00:32:35.060
columns that go next to
each other, as opposed to longer,

00:32:35.060 --> 00:32:37.220
into additional rows.

00:32:37.220 --> 00:32:40.790
We want to separate wider
this description column.

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:43.640
And we want the delimiter
to be the comma.

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:46.460
So I want something like this

00:32:46.460 --> 00:32:49.310
for one of my variables
and something like this for my

00:32:49.310 --> 00:32:53.150
other variable. And I'm going
to call those new variables

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:56.190
"species" and "island".

00:32:56.190 --> 00:33:00.120
This gives me something,
perhaps, a bit more useful than

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:03.550
these descriptions, but there's
a lot of repeated information.

00:33:03.550 --> 00:33:07.920
So if you are into that
sort of thing of writing regular expressions,

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:11.070
you can actually take things
one step further with a new

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:14.370
function called
separate_wider_regex(), where

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:20.970
you can actually give regular expression
patterns to it and, in one go,

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:24.390
result in something like a
species column and an island

00:33:24.390 --> 00:33:26.760
column without the
repeated information.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:28.950
If you are teaching
regular expressions,

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:30.720
I would say this
is the way to go.

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:33.660
If you're not teaching
regular expressions in your class--

00:33:33.660 --> 00:33:35.850
or before you teach
regular expressions--

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:38.610
you could do something
like this and then use some

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:42.120
functions from the
stringr package, like str_remove(),

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:46.260
to get rid of the redundant
species and island

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:48.750
tags here.

00:33:48.750 --> 00:33:52.150
Another exciting development here,
though, is something about

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:57.550
what happens when things
don't work out as expected.

00:33:57.550 --> 00:34:00.510
So if you look at the
documentation for these new

00:34:00.510 --> 00:34:06.540
separate() functions, you will see
some new arguments as well--

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:10.409
specifically, too_few
or too_many. So what to

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:13.889
do if there are too few
components? Should they be

00:34:13.889 --> 00:34:16.080
aligned at the start,
aligned at the end,

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:18.989
or should just give you an error
so that you can look into it?

00:34:18.989 --> 00:34:22.620
Or, if there are too
many of them, what to do?

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:23.520
Should they be dropped,

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:25.750
or should they be merged
with the last one?

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:29.969
And the debug option here
basically will print out something.

00:34:29.969 --> 00:34:32.699
It's probably not something
you want in your final code,

00:34:32.699 --> 00:34:36.060
but it's very helpful
for interactive use,

00:34:36.060 --> 00:34:39.120
where you can take
a look to see what

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:41.850
to do and then make a
decision in terms of whether

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:47.400
"align_start" or "align_end"
makes more sense.

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:50.040
We've talked about a lot
of new, exciting updates.

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:53.790
So I'd like to wrap this
up by talking about something maybe a

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:57.570
little bit more
lighthearted. So one minor

00:34:57.570 --> 00:34:59.220
update to ggplot2--

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:00.780
this is not the only
update, I think,

00:35:00.780 --> 00:35:02.190
that has happened to ggplot2,

00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:05.040
but one that continues to
catch me in my tracks--

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:07.920
is a new argument

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:09.360
for

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:10.440
changing

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:12.870
how your lines look,
so for line geoms.

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:14.820
So if I do something like this,

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:19.860
where I am plotting flipper
length versus body mass of penguins,

00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:23.520
and I want an overlay a smooth
curve over it but I want that

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:27.270
line to be a bit thicker,

00:35:27.270 --> 00:35:31.410
you will get a warning
that says please use linewidth

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:36.720
instead of size. I
do agree that linewidth indeed

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:39.780
seems to be more
appropriate here

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:43.020
in terms of what to call
that. And I've, in the past,

00:35:43.020 --> 00:35:46.770
struggled with articulating
why it was called size.

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:48.940
That being said,

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:51.120
I will say that

00:35:51.120 --> 00:35:53.490
the best teaching tip
I can give you is,

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:56.040
just check the output of
your old teaching materials

00:35:56.040 --> 00:35:58.530
thoroughly to not make a
fool of yourself when you're

00:35:58.530 --> 00:36:02.880
teaching to constantly see this warning
pop up at an unexpected time.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:06.900
So I've tried to go through
much of the code that is in the

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:11.640
accompanying blog post and interactively
explain some of these updates.

00:36:11.640 --> 00:36:15.450
I should mention and emphasize
that much of what I've talked

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:19.200
through are things that you
can now do optionally. And in

00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:22.050
places where I feel like
that is an improvement in the

00:36:22.050 --> 00:36:24.630
context of teaching, I
try to highlight those.

00:36:24.630 --> 00:36:28.620
You could also just not
do any of these things.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:31.950
Chances are none of your code
is going to fail, per se,

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:34.440
unless there was something
happening that was an error to

00:36:34.440 --> 00:36:36.660
begin with that was
slipping through.

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:41.430
But I think that these updates
are nice to think about as we

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:45.240
approach the new academic year and
as you might be thinking about

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:50.010
what to change or what
to update in your teaching materials.

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:53.370
And remember that the tidyverse blog
is a great place to catch

00:36:53.370 --> 00:36:57.140
up with all tidyverse
updates, whether teaching related or

00:36:57.140 --> 00:37:00.230
not. There's been a lot of
other updates to the packages,

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:03.320
that if you dig through this
year's post, that you will see--

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:06.530
to stringr, to
tidyr, to forcats--

00:37:06.530 --> 00:37:09.230
new API for the rvest package--

00:37:09.230 --> 00:37:16.040
and also, if you also tend to
teach SQL along with your data science content,

00:37:16.040 --> 00:37:19.790
shorter and more readable
and in some cases faster SQL

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:21.440
queries in dbplyr.

00:37:21.440 --> 00:37:25.160
And I'll wrap up by saying that
the new version of R

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:26.840
for Data
Science is out.

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:31.160
And much of the updates
that I've talked about here were

00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:34.520
sort of done in order to catch
up with what we wanted to

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:35.750
showcase in the book.

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:37.910
So you can expect the
development to slow down a

00:37:37.910 --> 00:37:41.570
little bit as you phase
in these new changes into your

00:37:41.570 --> 00:37:44.690
teaching materials. Thank
you very much for listening.

00:37:44.690 --> 00:37:45.590
[MUSIC PLAYING] PLAYING]

